  "worked_solution": "The mass $M$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=y{e}^{xy}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={\u222c}_{D}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:      \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{D}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}y[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{e}^{xy}]_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({e}^{y}-{e}^{0})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({e}^{y}-1)\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({e}^{y}-1)\\,\\text{d}y & =[{e}^{y}-y]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2}-2)-(1-0) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the plate is $M={e}^{2}-3.$",
